summary:
Proves the local Kudla–Rapoport conjecture (Theorem 3.4.1): for p > 2, an unramified quadratic extension F/F_0 of p-adic fields and a full-rank lattice L in the nonsplit hermitian space 𝕍_n, the arithmetic intersection number Int(L) of Kudla–Rapoport divisors on the unitary Rapoport–Zink space N_n of signature (1, n − 1) equals the central derivative ∂Den(L) of the local Siegel series. Consequences:
- Theorem 10.3.1, a variant at almost self-dual level; Theorem 10.5.1, conditional on Conjecture 10.4.1 of Kudla–Rapoport;
- the semi-global identity (Theorem 13.6.1);
- the global Kudla–Rapoport conjecture (Theorem 14.5.1, Corollary 14.5.2);
- with Liu's archimedean identity, the arithmetic Siegel–Weil formula for unitary Shimura varieties when F/F_0 is unramified at all finite places (Theorem 15.5.1).

The proof is by induction on n with an uncertainty principle from the Weil representation of SL_2. For fixed L^♭ of rank n − 1, the functions Int_{L^♭} and ∂Den_{L^♭} on 𝕍 \ L^♭_F split into horizontal and vertical parts:
- the horizontal parts agree through quasi-canonical lifting cycles (Theorems 4.2.1, 6.1.3);
- the geometric vertical part is Fourier-invariant up to γ_𝕍 = −1 (local modularity). This uses the Bruhat–Tits stratification by Deligne–Lusztig varieties and the Tate conjecture for them (Theorem 5.3.2, from Lusztig);
- the analytic vertical part has vanishing Fourier transform off the cone, via the Cho–Yamauchi formula;
- an induction on val(L^♭) closes the argument (Theorem 8.2.1).

The libraries have none of this. The atlas plans hermitian lattice classification (GN.2), Breuil–Kisin and Dieudonné theory (R07.4, R07.2), Grothendieck–Riemann–Roch (SF.5), cycle classes (MC.2), the Tate-conjecture statement (MC.7) and complex uniformization (V1).

Routes:
- Rapoport–Zink spaces, RSZ integral models and p-adic uniformization go to a Part II of PELModuli;
- Kudla–Rapoport cycles, the local and global identities and the arithmetic Siegel–Weil formula go to a Part II of GrossZagierAndArithmeticHeights;
- hermitian local densities go to GN.3, the Weil representation and uncertainty principle to MP.2/MP.3, and Tate's theorem to R07.1, all as sources.

Five source issues are recorded:
- a gap in the induction step of Theorem 8.2.1 (claimed equalities of vertical parts, repaired by comparing differences);
- a sign error in the Chern character of §6.4, which changes Lemmas 6.4.6–6.4.7 by (−1)^d but no theorem;
- wrong cross-references;
- V written for 𝕎_n and other misprints;
- a citation that attributes the general-n basis independence to Terstiege's n = 3 result.

routes.brief:
Start beyond Gross–Zagier formulas and arithmetic heights (GrossZagierAndArithmeticHeights). GZ.6–GZ.7 plan arithmetic generating series, incoherent kernels and local intersection identities for CM cycles on Shimura curves. This Part II does the same for unitary Shimura varieties of signature (n − 1, 1), following Li–Zhang, JAMS 35 (2022), 705–797.

Import:
- the Rapoport–Zink spaces, integral models and uniformization from UnitaryRapoportZinkSpacesAndRSZModels;
- hermitian local densities from GeometryOfNumbersAndQuadraticArithmetic GN.2–GN.3;
- the Fourier transform, Weil representations and uncertainty principle from MetaplecticAutomorphicForms MP.2–MP.3;
- Eisenstein series from AutomorphicSpectralTheory AS.1–AS.2, extended to Siegel degenerate principal series;
- Chern classes and Grothendieck–Riemann–Roch from SchemeAndStackFoundations SF.5; cycle classes (MC.2) and the Tate-conjecture statement (MC.7) from MotivesAndAlgebraicCycles;
- Tate modules and Breuil modules from FiniteFlatGroupsAndIntegralPadicHodgeTheory R07.1/R07.4, and étale cohomology from its existing owners.

Construct:
- Kudla–Rapoport cycles Z(L), K-theory with supports and derived intersections on formal schemes (Zhang App. B), and Int(L);
- the Bruhat–Tits stratification, Y_V and V(Λ) = Z(Λ);
- horizontal and vertical parts, and quasi-canonical lifting cycles;
- Lusztig's eigenvalues, the Deligne–Lusztig Tate theorem, and the local-modularity functions;
- the analytic functions ∂Den_{L^♭}, ∂Den_{L^♭,H}, ∂Den_{L^♭,V};
- the special divisors on N^1 and on Ñ^1 → Z(x_0);
- semi-global and global cycles Z(T, ϕ_K);
- Siegel Eisenstein series on U(n, n) with Whittaker factorization;
- Kudla Green currents and star products;
- Gillet–Soulé arithmetic degrees, shared with GSpinSpecialDivisorHeights if both are accepted.

The final theorems:
- Theorem 3.4.1 (Int(L) = ∂Den(L), p > 2), via Theorems 4.2.1, 5.3.2, 6.1.3, Corollary 6.3.3, Proposition 7.3.4, Theorem 7.4.1 and Theorem 8.2.1;
- Corollary 6.4.10 (higher local modularity);
- Theorem 10.3.1;
- Theorems 10.4.3 and 10.5.1, only under Conjecture 10.4.1, which the source assumes and which must stay an explicit hypothesis until proved;
- Theorem 13.6.1;
- Theorem 14.5.1 with Corollary 14.5.2 (Kudla–Rapoport's global conjecture);
- Theorem 15.3.1, imported from Liu or Garcia–Sankaran as a cited theorem;
- Theorem 15.5.1 (arithmetic Siegel–Weil formula when F/F_0 is unramified at all finite places and split above 2, with ϕ_K nonsingular at two split places).

Apply the corrections E1–E2:
- In the induction of Theorem 8.2.1, compare the differences Int_{L^♭,V} − ∂Den_{L^♭,V}, not the vertical parts separately.
- In §6.4, ch(V(Λ) ∩^L Z(x)) = ch(O_V) − ch(O(−Z(x))|_V), so Lemmas 6.4.6–6.4.7 carry (−1)^d. The local-modularity statements are unchanged.

Read KR11, KR14, Terstiege 2013, Vollaard–Wedhorn, Lusztig 1976 §§6–7, Cho–Yamauchi, Zhang 2021 App. B, Liu 2011 and Sankaran 2017 before closing.

Tests:
- n = 1, 2 against Examples 2.4.3 and 3.5.2;
- the n = 3 multiplicities of Theorem 5.4.1;
- Lemma 6.2.1's values;
- the Fourier evaluation of Lemma 6.3.1;
- Example 10.5.3.

routes.reason:
GN.3 constructs local representation densities, and GN.2 plans the hermitian variants of lattices and their local classification. This paper supplies the hermitian local-density package for an unramified quadratic extension:
- Den(M, L) and the normalized Siegel series Den(X, L) with its functional equation and cancellation laws;
- the Cho–Yamauchi weighted lattice count, with its finite-field isometry count and Lemma 1.9.1;
- the rank-one and special-value corollaries and the Terstiege–Katsurada induction formula;
- the almost self-dual variants (Lemma 9.1.1, Theorem 9.1.2, Den_Λ, Sankaran's rank-two formula);
- the lattice lemmas the analytic side uses (Lemmas 7.2.1–7.2.2 and 8.2.2, the notation of §7.1).
None of this involves Shimura varieties.

MP.2 owns the Weil index and the explicit Weil-representation formulas on unipotent, Levi and Fourier generators. MP.3 owns unitary dual pairs with their auxiliary characters. This source adds:
- the self-dual Fourier normalization on a hermitian space (1̂_L = vol(L)1_{L^∨});
- the SL_2 formulas and the Weil constant γ_V = −1 of a nonsplit hermitian space;
- the uncertainty principle, a direct consequence of those formulas (also used in Beuzart-Plessis's proof of the Jacquet–Rallis fundamental lemma);
- the U(n, n) × U(𝕍) Weil representation with its Siegel–Weil sections.

